          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you (in a sisterly way).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows affection for St. John, explicitly qualifying it as sisterly love rather than romantic or marital love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows sisterly affection but does not itself undertake marriage, travel, service, or any future obligation. In the surrounding turn Jane expressly denies being obligated to go to India; her separate statements about accompanying St. John must not be transferred onto the love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with strangers.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The words occur in a reasoned explanation distinguishing Jane's trust and sisterly affection from obligation or marital consent. They are not represented as an involuntary or reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression's core force is adequately represented as a qualified avowal of affection under T. Its conciliatory and relationship-defining context does not establish a separate core mode beyond that avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane speaks to St. John during a conflict over his demand that she marry him and accompany him to India. She rejects any binding obligation to go, but explains that she would have risked much specifically with him because she admires and trusts him and loves him as a sister. The expression therefore distinguishes sisterly affection from romantic love, marital consent, and missionary commitment.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no undertaking. Elsewhere Jane had offered to accompany St. John as an assistant or sister, but here she denies having made a formal promise or being obliged to go. St. John rejects the unmarried arrangement and does not treat the sisterly love avowal as acceptance of marriage; he instead turns to her attachment to Mr. Rochester.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre, including Jane's dispute with St. John Rivers and the concluding account of her marriage to Rochester.\",\"limitations\":\"The classification concerns the focal words within the supplied scene. The evidence clearly identifies the avowed affection as sisterly, but it does not provide vocal or performance details that would support an expressive-discharge reading.\"}"
        }
      ],
